#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116480 :tutorial/content "When we transform a logarithmic function $y = \\log_a (x),$ its domain might be modified as well.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function $f(x) = \\log_2(x-1).$ What is the domain of $f(x)?$\n\n![](images/f1/f135e52b40df0923dea0bfd58a3255a096cab4ab23faa7c761ac5998fdfdd782.png)\n\nFirst, recall that the domain of $y=\\log_a (x)$ is\n\n$$\nx > 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we take the above inequality and replace $x$ with the argument (or input) of the logarithm function contained in $f(x).$ In this case, the argument is $x-1,$ so we get\n\n$$\nx-1 > 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in(1, \\infty).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "345c9fab-a753-50d1-8833-12d8b2699fdc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2817 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116527 :tutorial/content "When we transform a logarithmic function $y = \\log_a (x),$ its range is not affected.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function $f(x) = 3\\log_2(x-1)+1.$ \n\n![](images/4a/4a5bad1159efe184b0afc2731ebd6b5dd6149a0a1ba2d530cfdc769ab2acf250.png)\n\nThe range of $\\log_2 x$ is\n\n$$\n\\log_2 x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nBecause the range of the logarithm function is the interval containing all real numbers, horizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect its range. Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c2975532-f898-5fd3-a901-a0d8f037906f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4589 :tutorial/title "The Range of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}]}
